Chapter: X509ParseTest
  cites Encode chapter X509
  cites Encode chapter Asn1
  cites Foreword chapter Ed25519
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe

 The certificate parser, on the certificate the IETF published in RFC 8410
 section 10.2 and the Ed25519 public key it published in section 10.1.

 Two published vectors, because one certificate cannot exercise both
 halves. The 10.2 certificate is SIGNED by an Ed25519 key but its own
 subject key is X25519 -- so it gates the certificate structure and the
 signature path, while 10.1, a standalone Ed25519 SubjectPublicKeyInfo,
 gates the Ed25519 key path. Neither half is checked against our own
 encoder, because we do not have one and do not want one.

 verify-tbs is again the decisive line. The signature is checked over
 xc-tbs -- the bytes the parser sliced out and kept -- so a parser that
 re-encoded the tbsCertificate, or took one byte too many, produces a
 message nobody signed and Ed25519 says False.

 can-verify-x25519 is the safety property. An X25519 key is a key
 AGREEMENT key. It can never verify a signature, and the type says so, so
 no caller can hand it to one by accident.

